82115932a201.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/da69cf085ea0816397356abf0878c798ab653c04fe34a51b8b849610d57778d4.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/5765a85c90552570f9a2a041f2f324a9fa0ce183d42708344531a13fbee802c8.png)\n\nConsequently,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/578802d62c9ab4cfbc8f250975b5b7be1396907593ba95901b8b21ad6a116d04.png)\n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/1c2480355dc4e29a136a3bd7b6855e076f1c9698507523cafbd934521b91c6bf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{4}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/c6714d0a6eaa5c7d354d46e8d6d60da7f3d2011df0fc75773036cf7ae510c5e3.png), find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/24878591902884f45f14f5c447bc326586a411a8870b205fc12825a4ab2c738a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/5c644b15efcf5a4274de23242a722604ead2ff800ea29ba7a650729227fefdf0.png) in terms of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/a7b9033c421072576aaf959980d778c319a8ed0f849e08db644b63bc6994088c.png) we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/166c90a9c29f99347bff6b397f31d705ec6407a19b4d71246f98308bbd7a5473.png) we want to match the expression\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/fc79367916dd7671c17de4f40b3d83f91d41c2f9988645964de0b53e5b11ab9b.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/91f686c6ca02e85c386cb0f9e80b66beface446d3a541952e0ac91738ad0db37.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/d54b9a8919c7b6106fcf5770c62b1823605d7be98a55d5333e73bcfb7528997a.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6e1bbbe9eb66856824ff70d16cc2c077519d361546b226a67146e20f15a24dd0.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/f1c944e6afa4e9cd84bc68004a2bfd918d81498a727aeb7c56ec627806bf93f6.png), we have:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/b7ac390c4e43b2fa2fbb4063632f98869256cea877caa5e5dad4aad33ae94722.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/7e5f2b01647ab01052fff18ab760273eebe19d47b56051b1953adf587d7f9fb3.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/784225d11bf94b7fbd67f8e2395695141eb75b1cb44f89d49b29bb3a2031534b.png) have the same direction\n\nHence, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/21261c8ad0d86a9e7300ffc58c57fd76beef9f627492bcfe2051d5e3cfd82a4e.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/ae9c98ded3aa4317a729571710ad38d973a2d67b5faeb9ad26af7c24ee5c3193.png)\n\nConsequently, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/aee3287ad64a654880216142756811bd4d5232b3dba2f48378b8b1c5241e4316.png)\n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/4ac145b710c5354b44a4ea1dc820d70f54b5c17985f078fce6313b671ec26fd9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221e771d-84c4-50b1-8618-44ebdc675980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384329} {:answer/id #uuid "c6dc3fc8-a383-56cc-97eb-d74336f193e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384330} {:answer/id #uuid "1e19d4d4-cdd3-53d1-b01a-f40dc5dbdda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384331} {:answer/id #uuid "1c425f8a-c4c7-5ffe-a914-7041fa679ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384332} {:answer/id #uuid "918426dd-0878-5352-ac59-cbd210bc5acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e19d4d4-cdd3-53d1-b01a-f40dc5dbdda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384331} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a5a4e3d-6e41-5902-8b0b-ae4a1abde016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d6bdb0f-98b1-5fd8-99e9-8c3cc74682e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-120244" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/9e595809380e3067934b9f8c86933dd5300fcfb54ce4202eddc0e4196b74cf00.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/0c785e189beebb0a803bceba272db156af6d3b47c690ee9527876f77c65d1891.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a687ab2-ea90-5824-9ba5-4ed8ae14f802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310723} {:answer/id #uuid "63c0c53b-c2d7-5f06-bdd1-d1443d16baac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310724} {:answer/id #uuid "9ace3fd4-7686-501e-9cab-130766debd44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310725} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0a54b2-2c7a-558c-887a-0a1efb1bc99d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310726} {:answer/id #uuid "294d056a-3875-53ac-ae2b-b67f67226abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ace3fd4-7686-501e-9cab-130766debd44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310725} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7629b1f-33ae-51af-8a50-8de25ffe6a15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c6d0a6c5-043c-5c21-9b3d-b465d593098a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120260" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/73e0ac67304b5e7682abddb59fcd01a9e8aca88831a303bf5b31a72ff772228d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-3b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/2484cf9fdda85c5c516babe6814f34c369ebdd91d6f100f0ce0fe4102e3c90a6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-3b=-3a-3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7614bf0-1e7c-574b-b719-2688014f48d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322254} {:answer/id #uuid "bae8a6ed-0e5a-5cdf-b3ed-a8910f140309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322255} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb2d506-793c-5a3d-b8ac-0efc2d553542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322256} {:answer/id #uuid "905c6c4e-3fe6-5f97-a292-9c43daa5e840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322257} {:answer/id #uuid "785a5215-204e-5370-b63f-8281b34f4e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "905c6c4e-3fe6-5f97-a292-9c43daa5e840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322257} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d4f01ec-b054-5eed-bf09-91ea80a8787c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49e0b1b0-d9e2-5f20-a04d-af2c1aeb9c97" :question/math-academy-id "q-120268" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/25717d521feeec25f7b4b722ca7b8a4ee9b5fdc8423bac06953e5f2adc8eef3d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/d585c949d7de8cc3fa2d62bfb60990af12242c32ef3390e563f7e627844c5a88.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=3|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ and $r$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=3r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =3r-2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=3r-2s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c2a97e3-e0a9-5f32-bcd1-1aa110198dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310730} {:answer/id #uuid "00b326a5-5d20-5dbe-ad5f-602a0161eade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310731} {:answer/id #uuid "f76afb29-4f8b-5c3d-ac84-11f2c034d54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310732} {:answer/id #uuid "ed01b7ff-cbb3-57f4-9fda-cbc71952ee9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310733} {:answer/id #uuid "c47cdc39-faa1-5a79-acfc-b375a31d9ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f76afb29-4f8b-5c3d-ac84-11f2c034d54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310732} :answer-field/id #uuid "648263a5-2cb8-5739-b310-df939d97e60a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "579d00a6-a778-595a-a8fe-d160cda52449" :question/math-academy-id "q-120279" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/fc7d37da99173b7fa99f2f5f0bbf1478a54505628ed827488a18b3d01535f81f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6c9273c0b89d4c06c8a8591b8e8e94b0c83b2d1d4bd04b64ed8cb0a185619b5f.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=2|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2k.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-j-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-j-2k.$\n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b26e8dc-381e-5cd8-bd1f-6e5ce6c2a927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{1}{3}u+\\frac{2}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384336} {:answer/id #uuid "4520e6dd-1096-51b7-902f-810bba3287fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{2}{9}u+\\frac{7}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384337} {:answer/id #uuid "119bbc1f-77cb-5f6e-841f-a7461d0af059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{5}{7}u+\\frac{2}{7}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384338} {:answer/id #uuid "eff802b1-731c-51b7-bf9a-9e37d4effaf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384339} {:answer/id #uuid "645d11af-3b90-5cd4-bcae-747f6464c24d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{2}{3}u+\\frac{1}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4520e6dd-1096-51b7-902f-810bba3287fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{2}{9}u+\\frac{7}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384337} :answer-field/id #uuid "44fd3dc6-4a28-5719-899d-335c26b0b275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "426f1843-d9cb-5293-a83b-9e7101ceab9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120294" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/707beca1d856414e25874cc0b56c6654fbb41d699f01106b6749d49408b39aee.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=7:2.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=7:2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{7}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{7}{9}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{7}{9}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}u+\\frac{7}{9}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f89674-d7af-5dc8-bfd9-2e087787aab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{2}{3}m+\\frac{1}{3}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322261} {:answer/id #uuid "72a56613-fe00-52b3-b362-68b5b14d6ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{1}{2}m+\\frac{1}{2}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322262} {:answer/id #uuid "f98b29bb-af53-5033-85b6-2886693498ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{5}{9}m+\\frac{4}{9}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322263} {:answer/id #uuid "5adaac84-a815-509a-81a5-14473e6256df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{1}{3}m+\\frac{2}{3}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322264} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a1fce0-ac36-5b88-bd9c-087cb7e5f75e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{4}{9}m+\\frac{5}{9}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5adaac84-a815-509a-81a5-14473e6256df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{1}{3}m+\\frac{2}{3}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322264} :answer-field/id #uuid "c63444f6-473b-524b-9370-90dd340d3013" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b737947-533d-5a00-ac54-8d960f9a17b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120303" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/50b87e86bd2985e54ce0a8fe6b3e23986f7e1946a5284d0ae297d96b9cbc60f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=m,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=n$ and $P$ divides $\\overset{―}{MN}$ in the ratio $MP:PN=2:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OP}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=m,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MP}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-m+n \\\\  & =n-m.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MP:PN=2:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MP}=\\frac{2}{3}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{2}{3}(n-m).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP} \\\\  & =m+\\frac{2}{3}(n-m) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}m+\\frac{2}{3}n.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91ca9633-13a0-5b3f-9d35-131a010c80d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094893} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0f1ec8-8c82-5d98-872e-6808eff3d9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094894} {:answer/id #uuid "40dc8516-4025-5009-a188-9129456e510f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094895} {:answer/id #uuid "5def0dfa-eda1-5673-ac26-4345f4090725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094896} {:answer/id #uuid "658e6611-ce0a-5829-bf6f-1cd2bfcc5908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5def0dfa-eda1-5673-ac26-4345f4090725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094896} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee69212-ba9f-5d68-9bb7-ec1de464bc9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "767cbe5b-8f45-54bf-8422-666ebc7778f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-19338" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/816c4ecbfe49321bc6b2bc0d72eb61d6ddd1aeae7e76a275df0975439dde5865.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6782f2fa59056afd9022edbaf69c44a991bf12a7ac6a190bea3cc08fc7a4996c.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2.5|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.5b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2.5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2.5b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2.5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c520cb51-5072-578f-9ef2-7ca1df420256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095001} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfd29ce-4f2f-528b-852c-456eb428821c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095002} {:answer/id #uuid "e3196dd8-ce19-597e-a7ff-2081788710e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095003} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8cd77c-fd8c-5d9e-97c1-9713f4e875e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095004} {:answer/id #uuid "357c5561-c74d-5098-b3a2-2c48e7ec2bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c520cb51-5072-578f-9ef2-7ca1df420256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095001} :answer-field/id #uuid "4462ed2a-506b-54ad-abdd-310a36757e16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a4f66a0-1cfd-52d9-be40-ea47c3b77cf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-20435" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/4b7c8c679171c1dcb6ef44b33696ba2ccce87b3f690643af979476d8dfb285ce.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/f67af4512a7ab61f862932a60dafc25ef62bc46335b7d7df323fe889f8728e6b.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=\\frac{1}{2}a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "558d0456-17ca-5723-a2a6-0f711767c356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "558d0456-17ca-5723-a2a6-0f711767c356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310744} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b3129e6-0e85-53b4-8d5e-6c29575dc17f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1064db4-89c9-5bbf-882b-ee2e96404aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-275120" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/1d637a841739052b4e2d26f89bbcfd92eefefcebf6000a46a10a45b7e53ecf15.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+3b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/33a5661bc10830c7e52e2ba7f27779a28c6e5f9cf39630e60d0634f3b6f7ccdd.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9891f941-47e3-5915-8ca6-8f696a84c886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384343} {:answer/id #uuid "62a91400-1394-5c71-bf24-68c563c8b75a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62a91400-1394-5c71-bf24-68c563c8b75a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384344} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1579070-7bd6-5ee9-b6a5-af394ba37133" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384342} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b3da92b-7081-5cf5-9dd9-6a96c9149ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b3da92b-7081-5cf5-9dd9-6a96c9149ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384346} :answer-field/id #uuid "c945b93c-4002-5736-9f1a-0e431bb90f15" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3088d475-ce58-52b9-ba8a-49f9f22bb4ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-278263" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/c26ec7e1fccc5ada4c43eef55faf9e4fa3e17ed896b1546f472edf744404da1a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OE}=e,$ and $F$ divides $\\overset{―}{DE}$ in the ratio $DF:FE=2:3.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OF}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{DF}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ODE,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\overset{\\to }{DO}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $DF:FE=2:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\frac{2}{5}\\overset{\\to }{DE}=\\frac{2}{5}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OF} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{2}{5}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}d+\\frac{2}{5}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92dd2734-6dae-57ff-9b06-71ab3dfc49b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92dd2734-6dae-57ff-9b06-71ab3dfc49b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384349} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f009688-4ef9-5753-ba65-afdb8e806572" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ae27f2-85cc-5f94-abf0-7351c9beca1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ae27f2-85cc-5f94-abf0-7351c9beca1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384351} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ab7ba89-c18c-5247-9d73-1677eb687356" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4379c333-620d-5f21-90a0-ba71841120f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-322575" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/d9448f9f05666f9806975b37a73f09c492eb73139b6f9395b619f077f3a584c9.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q,$ and $R$ divides $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ in the ratio $PR:RQ=4:9.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ via the vectors $p$ and $q.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OR}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$p+$\n{{field-2}}\n$q$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OR}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{PR}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OPQ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{PO}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-p+q \\\\  & =q-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $PR:RQ=4:9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PR}=\\frac{4}{13}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=\\frac{4}{13}(q-p).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =p+\\frac{4}{13}(q-p) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{13}p+\\frac{4}{13}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb4bdfa5-e724-56eb-8a6a-3f16a9657d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384354} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef43215-1609-556a-bda3-e621b529e54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb4bdfa5-e724-56eb-8a6a-3f16a9657d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384354} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c35eb38-4703-518c-b405-b36d99040135" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384353} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd8cc461-184d-5e91-8c59-547fb0957017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8cc461-184d-5e91-8c59-547fb0957017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384357} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f0359dd-750d-5338-8f37-8e574097093b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6c54d99-928f-590e-afa1-598de1a85379" :question/math-academy-id "q-322586" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/1deea2da05b0d12c7e9afbb12f0340bdbfef18ff37dc49c506929028bfeb67c4.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OL}=l,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p,$ and $Q$ divides $\\overset{―}{LP}$ in the ratio $LQ:QP=7:9.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OQ}$ via the vectors $l$ and $p.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$l+$\n{{field-2}}\n$p$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{LQ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OL}=l,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{LQ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OLP,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LP} & =\\overset{\\to }{LO}+\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =-l+p \\\\  & =p-l.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $LQ:QP=7:9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{LQ}=\\frac{7}{16}\\overset{\\to }{LP}=\\frac{7}{16}(p-l).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{LQ} \\\\  & =l+\\frac{7}{16}(p-l) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}l+\\frac{7}{16}p.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d340a3d6-73b6-50ab-9cd4-75007d4de8ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374586} {:answer/id #uuid "2df8c6e0-270d-5147-80a9-af3f7a3e1a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2df8c6e0-270d-5147-80a9-af3f7a3e1a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374587} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e73a2f7-1c85-5bd8-8efd-9ce3338efc35" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8a903e8-340b-59c1-b98b-e4da25996e2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-325974" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/bae20e625c529ef2157faa4d534a19529c605c9002ed750e56723ff29e265226.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-4b,$ then\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-4b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-4b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-4b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/139a4d4312ecbe42cd3a3859af4f4d7b93f8bbc54548ac5a0244c04e7ba5287b.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-4b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-4b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-4b=-3a-4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d0f419-57de-547f-8d8e-54d210b142fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310760} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e5b9db-0b7c-56ca-af3e-896e2c8ee0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4e5b9db-0b7c-56ca-af3e-896e2c8ee0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310761} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7be7c3-ef06-563e-90a1-fdd90054876a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ca5f72c-bdbb-5dac-8df8-acc9530dfad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ca5f72c-bdbb-5dac-8df8-acc9530dfad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310763} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fae648c-fad5-586a-9449-3fa74de70bd7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d681eea-b9ef-5eca-b425-7d37983545ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-326013" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/586fb01cd796c9d4e04aaeb393ea7f5fadd6c836d0b39d36c9c668447c490bd1.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/2d3a5c31af776cc49c2af118edc7256ca533320c7d53245593e5de6649ce03cf.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $u$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2v.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-u-2v.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v=-1u-2v.$\n\nSince $v∦u,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1881c0f3-cd34-5af6-b500-f3259283a978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1881c0f3-cd34-5af6-b500-f3259283a978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384360} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d014524-58d3-529c-a22b-f6b9b63acb49" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384359} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cce72f04-5ebd-5154-bf57-a0ac41573377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce72f04-5ebd-5154-bf57-a0ac41573377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384362} :answer-field/id #uuid "59582d9e-7546-51c2-acfd-74c2eabbf30b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "126ed0a4-da79-50b6-9c3c-a0a12a9343df" :question/math-academy-id "q-327074" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/c8cbd5b93633e4e4899a1cde0da618f3be792bdbbd77db650c32814187b222f8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6708537b79154a7778fe52e8d82b6577f93d40a06d3af0267e3e4b7eaa6468a9.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OM=3|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-3q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=2p.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-3q+2p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p-3q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a49739fd-5d64-5072-991b-0e2de77044c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384365} {:answer/id #uuid "9706540e-ce36-5d80-9a63-097023b2bead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9706540e-ce36-5d80-9a63-097023b2bead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384366} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3e5725-87bb-5907-a975-4d9a4d0f162e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "006dbbba-1a21-56cc-a22e-dea7b7e3886d" :question/math-academy-id "q-367059" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/9d117a1429a5d5264ca08bfae899086d821b37a84ec03921347f8e0fb207343f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/8daf3d00afbe4afe9678853f4fec50dd0861ca9a36a6dae96f8fa2d288c45be1.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=-2a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dfbda2c-c6ad-5b26-bbe6-3600d0bd3da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dfbda2c-c6ad-5b26-bbe6-3600d0bd3da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384369} :answer-field/id #uuid "cba97d6d-df0e-5a15-be93-3841e19ccb00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efda2e30-b380-5dd9-8c2f-b65628de7f34" :question/math-academy-id "q-367061" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/508d40546c76dfe015a0c1a40aeb3960f08ed78a72333daa155fcecee19e481c.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/2e85e0495bb2f1ed7349ed511d06c87b924f5414f03c0d5c907afe63ad9a948c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=-3a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c328b4b7-da8e-5a4f-ae5f-7584bce68984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c328b4b7-da8e-5a4f-ae5f-7584bce68984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322274} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f09979-820c-588c-a537-148693d26410" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65028b3c-06a7-5ff4-b830-82c5ce361a6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-367064" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/80cedeceb8d377617af880cab1c383643e3cd79055eccce046b0418598897ac8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/7e6923e8b8b774eeaa2f885ec6aa24bb03f07c547fa50e7145a4113a18e92e8a.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-2a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208efaab-2244-5ad9-ba54-968db33be116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384372} {:answer/id #uuid "0d41c933-e27f-5d90-aa62-553ba514bbf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384373} {:answer/id #uuid "8c197631-0385-5be5-8c82-bb1e66244c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384374} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2dea85-a020-55f5-b7dd-b26cdcc4c2c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384375} {:answer/id #uuid "6c20faa9-4dc5-59cb-8962-551d32ed860c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c20faa9-4dc5-59cb-8962-551d32ed860c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384376} :answer-field/id #uuid "054227d2-a55b-53e9-8044-05d6a8a46e5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "903beb22-87ab-5040-ba13-1ea1a5064be5" :question/math-academy-id "q-42676" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/be4bd3ff63baa05d861cbad6a26c6b53b8b7f1fed64feb0c0465408c6e7ced2d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/a216faf7db26da01730a18607a298e442af47a7d45a7a69e6fd12cbfd2229360.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=3|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $j$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-3j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $k$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=k.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-3j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-3j+k.$\n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =1$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51bd9973-499c-5ed9-8608-ca62370e0194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384379} {:answer/id #uuid "403ab06d-573b-5406-bbe2-2794009580ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384380} {:answer/id #uuid "2ebc104d-db7c-5c94-9ae5-ded04f668718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384381} {:answer/id #uuid "1948f3fb-b4a0-52c5-a964-9ec8c2c4e6d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384382} {:answer/id #uuid "caf987ab-f167-5bf1-a9fa-061916aad5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "403ab06d-573b-5406-bbe2-2794009580ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384380} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b963f19-5852-5d5b-9108-b5e43ba083ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07e219e0-84ab-586e-9173-3f68d3a4fc62" :question/math-academy-id "q-70140" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/7578bb2e5b594161744d96d918fa75e0225e723138d010dba3ed635098e75ca2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/4797ffa54b3c2e4d177a6c3d527b1aaf3f1460d3c509bca86395e5f360314d25.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=3|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"}]}
